; 06-var.kurt

; `var` explicitly declares a symbol as a variable: something that may stand
; for arbitrary objects, as opposed to one fixed constant (05-const.kurt).

var x, y

; Just like `const`, once a role is fixed on a level it cannot be changed
; afterwards, in either direction:
;
;     const x           ; would fail: `x` is already a variable

; Symbols starting with a dollar sign, e.g. `$A` or `$x`, are *always*
; variables, no matter what -- they mark the parts of a `use` axiom that may
; be matched against anything.  Every theory file is full of them, e.g.
; `minimal.kurt` contains `use $A implies $A "restatement"`.  Symbols
; starting with `%`, e.g. `%A`, are the same idea specialised to whole
; boolean formulas rather than plain terms -- you'll see plenty of those once
; we `load prop` in lesson 10.
